On 8 July 1982, [[West Germany national football team|West Germany]] and [[France national football team|France]] played in the semi-finals of the [[1982 FIFA World Cup]] at the [[Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán Stadium]] in [[Seville]], [[Spain]]. The match is known in both countries as the '''Night of Seville''' ({{langx|de|Nacht von Sevilla}},<ref>{{cite web |last1=Klemm |first1=Stephan |title=Die Nacht von Sevilla |url=https://www.fr.de/sport/fussball/nacht-sevilla-11230730.html |website=fr.de |date=15 January 2019 |publisher=Frankfurter Rundschau |access-date=8 May 2020}}</ref> {{langx|fr|Nuit de Séville}}<ref>{{cite web |last1=Delerm |first1=Philippe |title=Et le meilleur de notre jeunesse s'est envolé |url=https://www.lefigaro.fr/vox/societe/2014/07/04/31003-20140704ARTFIG00146-philippe-delerm-et-le-meilleur-de-notre-jeunesse-s-est-envole.php |website=lefigaro.fr |date=4 July 2014 |publisher=FigaroVox |access-date=8 May 2020}}</ref>).

The match was won by West Germany 5–4 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]]. They advanced to face [[Italy national football team|Italy]] in the final. Thanks to its back-and-forth drama, four goals in extra time and a dramatic penalty shoot-out, the match is regarded as one of the best football matches of all time.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Ger|first1=McCarthy|title=Memory Lane – West Germany v France at World Cup 82|date=10 July 2011 |url=https://backpagefootball.com/memory-lane-west-germany-v-france-at-world-cup-82/21132/|publisher=Backpage Football|access-date=17 June 2014}}</ref> It is considered by French captain [[Michel Platini]] to be his "most beautiful game."<ref name="pears">{{cite web|last1=Pears|first1=Tim|title=My most beautiful game|url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2008/oct/26/world-cup-platini|work=The Guardian|date=26 October 2008|access-date=17 June 2014}}</ref> West Germany's victory was the first time in the history of the World Cup whose outcome was determined by a penalty shoot-out;<ref>{{cite web|last1=Murray|first1=Scott|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/may/27/world-cup-25-stunning-moments-patrick-battiston|title=World Cup: 25 stunning moments: Patrick Battiston loses his teeth|date=27 May 2014|work=The Guardian|access-date=2 April 2015}}</ref> while the penalty shoot-out rule was already in place since the [[1974 FIFA World Cup]], it had not been used in the four matches that would require it in the two tournaments prior (the matches for third place and the finals).

==Venue==
[[File:Estadio Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán Preferencia and Gol Norte-2007-04-05.jpg|thumb|left|200px|Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán Stadium, venue of the match]]
The match was held in [[Ramón Sánchez-Pizjuán Stadium]], home stadium of [[Sevilla Fútbol Club|Sevilla FC]] and named after the club's former president, [[Ramón Sánchez-Pizjuán]] (1900–1956).<ref>{{cite web|url =https://www.laliga.com/en-GB/news/ten-things-you-may-not-know-about-the-ramon-sanchez-pizjuan|title=Ten things you may not know about the Ramón Sánchez-Pizjuán|publisher=La Liga|date=21 July 2015|access-date=28 February 2021}}</ref>

Sánchez-Pizjuán Stadium is nicknamed ''La Bombonera'', more commonly used to refer to [[La Bombonera|Estadio Alberto J. Armando]], the home stadium of [[Boca Juniors]].<ref name=boca>{{cite web|url=https://www.bocajuniors.com.ar/el-club/la-bombonera |title=La Bombonera {{!}} El club |work=bocajuniors.com.ar |publisher=CA Boca Juniors |access-date=10 June 2018 |language=es}}</ref><ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20150402112020/http://www.pagina12.com.ar/diario/deportes/8-120059.html «La Bombonera» inspira temor], ''Página/12''</ref> The [[Spain national football team|Spain national team]] has never lost a game against an international team in this stadium. Opened in 1958, the stadium has capacity for 42,714 spectators.<ref>{{cite web|title=Sevilla F.C. The organization|website=www.sevillafc.es|url=https://www.sevillafc.es/en/the-club/organization/sevilla-fc|access-date=31 January 2022}}</ref>
